This video is sponsored by Book Walker.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.840
If something happens that forces you to confront your own mortality...

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:12.040
Where do you even start with that?

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:14.660
Who can you even turn to?

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.880
That's not a question I hope any of you will have to face in the near future,

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:22.860
as I'm sure most of us are pretty content with just living our daily lives.

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:26.320
But if something like that were to happen,

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:32.660
in the face of death, how desperately would you search for that meaning in your life?

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:36.339
((SFX: Rewind)) Wow, that was a really morbid start to the video...

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:38.700
You know, you know, I'm sure there's a better way to put this.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:39.700
Michael, you explain it!

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:42.260
Hey! Vsauce, Michael here.

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:43.780
When will you die?

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:46.180
Today, I wanted to talk about "I Want to Eat Your Pancreas",

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:48.540
which is a story that explores exactly that.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:50.760
A cute anime girl searching for a meaning in life,

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:52.040
just before her own death.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:55.420
And I would call that massive spoilers if it didn't open up with her damn funeral.

00:00:55.629 --> 00:01:00.728
This is a title that comes in the form of an anime, a manga, or a light novel, and a live-action movie!

00:01:00.729 --> 00:01:01.719
So, yeah!

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:03.220
Just... Just take your pick really.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:06.080
Do you like animation? Pictures? Words?

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:10.780
Or weird creatures who inhabit this strange world that looks like a less aesthetic Makoto Shinkai movie.

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:13.440
Now, for those of you who have never heard of this anime before,

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.940
I'm sure there's a bunch of raised eyebrows of what the title of this movie implies.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:20.820
As in the medium where just every niche kink is accounted for.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:25.220
One wouldn't be blamed for thinking: "Finally, the anime I was waiting for!".

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:29.140
A story about the human desire to consume the insulin organ!

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:32.880
But fortunately, this anime has nothing to do with the consumption of body parts

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:36.180
and joins the ranks of "Rascal Does Not Dream of Bunny Girl Senpai".

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:38.640
In the latest trend of anime titles trying their best

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.900
to literally clickbait an audience into watching them.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:45.120
So, three years down the line. Don't be surprised if posters start looking like this.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.240
The story actually revolves around two people,

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:49.720
The cheerful Sakura and our gloomy main boy.

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:51.420
- Who I can't name due to small spoilers -

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:54.960
Who one day by chance, happens upon Sakura's diary in a hospital.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.760
Wholesomely titled, "Living with Dying".

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:01.700
Where she explains to him that she's a normal girl living her pretty normal life,

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:04.100
where she goes to school and hangs out with friends,

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:05.800
and doesn't do anything out of the ordinary.

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:08.460
Oh! Also, she has the C A N C E R.

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:11.740
Well, it's never actually explicitly stated that it's cancer,

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:13.940
and refer to it as some sort of "pancreatic disease".

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:17.260
But who the hell is knowledgeable about terminal pancreatic diseases?

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:20.560
It's not exactly the S-tier power ranking of the terminal illness world,

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.200
where like lung and liver are the cancer rockstars with all the attention they get.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:26.820
Upon finding out this fact, he agrees to keep it a secret.

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:29.040
As she hasn't told any of her close friends,

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:32.920
and through this shared secret, they find a small bond that slowly grows.

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:36.460
As we follow the duo in the final months leading up to Sakura's death,

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:38.880
and the precious time they spend with each other,

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:41.680
as she searches for a conclusion in her life.

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:43.660
The quote: "I Want To Eat Your Pancreas",

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:47.480
comes from an old superstition that if there was something wrong with a certain body part,

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:50.300
if you eat that same body part then it'll get better.

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:51.940
So that's where the name comes from.

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:52.840
Nothing too ridiculous.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:56.080
But in my mind, I still find it amusing that some non-anime fans out there,

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.160
will be searching for the lyrics to "I want to break free" on Google

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:01.140
when they randomly see: "What the fu-"

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:04.520
Terminal illnesses isn't exactly a topic that's new in film and television.

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:07.320
Given that it's a pretty easy issue to revolve drama around.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:10.400
Someone has a disease that will cause them to die?

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:11.560
Then they die.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:12.680
And it's sad!

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:13.700
Death is Sad.

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:14.460
Who knew?

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:19.280
So many aspects can now be seen as clichéd given the popularity of teen cancer romance dramas

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:20.380
like "The Fault in Our Stars".

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:22.320
Which tries its best to tug at my heartstrings,

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:24.740
but I can never quite shake the feeling of points

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:27.980
that the writing seemed more garnered towards the preteen twilight demographic.

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:30.080
Which is why it never fully connected with me.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:31.980
"I should probably go to sleep."

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:32.920
*deep sigh*

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:33.880
"Okay."

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:34.620
"OK."

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:36.720
"Okay."

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:37.580
"oK."

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:40.480
((Distorted)) Perhaps "OK" will be our "Always"

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:42.480
"OKAY."

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:45.540
So how does Pancreas separate itself from its predecessors?

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:47.300
Well, on the surface, not much.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:48.840
I mean if you look at the plot description,

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:50.800
it does have its clichés.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:55.280
To the point where you'd be able to spell out the basic plot points in some cheesy early 2000s trailer voice like:

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:00.080
"I have cancer."

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:01.080
"That's cool, I don't."

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.760
"Oh my god, we have like NOTHING in common!"

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:10.300
"I know you're like, dying. And-... And stuff."

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:12.660
"But, wou-... would you go to homecoming with me?"

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:13.340
*giggle*

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:14.820
"Of course I will."

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:16.340
"I wanna eat your pancreas!"

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:18.720
"Wait, wait-... Wait, wait. What-"

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:20.220
"What the hell did you just say?"

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:24.780
"Perhaps I wanna eat your pancreas will be our always-"

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:28.060
So, there's nothing overly complex I can really say about the plot.

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:31.740
Aside from one thing, the movie plays out pretty much exactly the way you think it would,

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:34.160
and even though there isn't an attempt to shake things up.

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:36.940
It doesn't change anything of consequence in the grand scheme of things.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:40.600
But, I thought it worked in spite of this because of subtleties.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:45.080
This could have easily divulged into the cheesy romance cancer drama we've all seen before.

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:47.420
But one of the aspects I enjoyed was the fact

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:49.780
that the relationships that was explored between our leads

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:52.520
was way more complex than purely a romantic one.

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:54.160
Sure, there's a bit of romance involved.

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:56.920
But this isn't the tragic story of the socially awkward guy

00:04:56.920 --> 00:04:59.060
finding the dying Cancer Bae love of his dreams.

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:02.080
It's far more focused on human connection and what we yearn for

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:03.960
with our relationships with other people.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:07.400
And, the dynamic between our leads is what this is entirely focused around.

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:09.180
They are the backbone of everything.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:10.920
The entire story revolves around them,

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:12.160
and they are what the movie,

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:13.760
excuse the morbidly accurate pun:

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:15.060
"LIVES AND DIES BY"

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:18.780
The story portrays them as polar opposites that intrinsically balance each other out.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:21.660
Our main guy is someone who doesn't care for other people.

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:24.360
So much so, he's never had anything close to resembling a friend

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:26.680
or even a personal connection with anyone.

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:29.760
While Sakura is the confident, outspoken anime girl,

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:31.780
whose never afraid to share her mind.

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:34.960
Yes, he was an introvert. She was an extrovert.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:37.220
Can I make it any more obvious?

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:40.300
It might not be a new idea to have two polar opposite people

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:44.980
form an unlikely friendship, but the character development here is handled absolutely beautifully.

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:47.580
That, I wouldn't have it any other way.

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:50.080
What more can I say?

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:51.320
(Okay sorry, I'll stop doing that now.)

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:55.850
She is his first real connection he's ever had and he brings her a sense of normalcy in her life.

00:05:55.850 --> 00:06:00.060
But while there might be the initial reason they hang out, you see a subtle shift

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:04.800
as they slowly start to depend on each other, influence each other, and without noticing it,

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:07.220
reach a point where they aspire to be like one another.

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:10.320
He learns the value of forming a bond with other people

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:14.860
and she envies the ability to have your own allure without the need for anyone else.

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:17.040
Together, there's a sense of completion.

00:06:17.060 --> 00:06:19.220
Like, two sides of the same coin.

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:21.820
Never quite able to grasp to the other side

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:25.640
yet intrinsically linked forever in an existence of perfect balance.

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:29.620
In the end, the relationship they share isn't one of romantic partners.

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:31.900
But, it's not one of friendship either.

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:34.380
It's a complex connection that's hard to put into words.

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:39.440
Though suffice to say, they become two people very important to each other who also rely on each other.

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:44.720
And, it's in seeing the connection form, and the beautiful imprint it was able to leave on both of them,

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:49.640
that in the end, was a huge factor that moved me to the point of tears.

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:52.460
Because despite the fact I've been using humor in this video,

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:54.980
and more or less talked about everything in a lighthearted manner.

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:58.000
I'd be lying if I said it didn't hit me.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:06:59.220
Because it did.

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:02.400
The thing I related to was the main character's journey.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:07.540
As someone who grew up as that awkward anti-social kid who'd rather spend all his energy watching anime

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:08.800
rather than making friends.

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:13.740
Seeing someone find the beauty of discovering that right connection to another person

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:15.980
certainly made me reflect on every life

00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:19.980
I had encountered that shaped me into the best person I think I am today.

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:23.940
And how every one of them had left their own mark on me in some way.

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:27.640
It's when I started to understand what the film was trying to say

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:32.300
that the huge elephant in the room, slowly crept its way out of the shadows.

00:07:32.380 --> 00:07:34.040
Into plain sight.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:36.280
She's going to die.

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:40.620
And, it's only a matter of time before it happens.

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:43.780
Call it a trope, call it a cliché.

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:46.200
But it doesn't change the gravity of that fact.

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:49.500
Whether it'd be in real life or in a fictional story.

00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:52.560
Knowing this, we start to mirror the main character,

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:55.820
we erect towards, prevent ourselves from getting hurt.

00:07:55.820 --> 00:08:00.960
And yet, as they spend more time with each other and you cautiously open yourself up.

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:02.580
Something seems off.

00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:06.700
It's not the fact she seems like someone who's going to die.

00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:08.900
It's the fact that she doesn't.

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:13.880
There's just something about her infectious charm that makes you impossible not to fall into her flow.

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:15.540
You know you shouldn't grow attached.

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:18.320
But, as the finites runtime presses on.

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:20.740
Like him, you realize: "Crap."

00:08:20.740 --> 00:08:23.360
You don't want that inevitable thing to happen.

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:25.560
And even though you know it's going to,

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:28.540
in the moment as you see her joyfully smile.

00:08:29.260 --> 00:08:32.060
It just doesn't feel real.

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:52.180
What truly moved me with this movie wasn't her impending fate.

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:54.300
For a film about terminal illness,

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:58.440
little was actually spotlighted about the physical toll of living with that illness.

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:02.360
Instead it was seeing just how much a life can change a person.

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:06.000
How the right connection can give you the conclusion you were looking for.

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:09.140
Because despite the topic that the plot revolves around,

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:11.980
this isn't a story about dying.

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:15.960
It's one about finding out what it means to be living.

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:18.740
I'm sure many of us now won't be in a stage,

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:22.220
where you have to ponder about something as gloomy as our own mortality.

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:24.960
But whether it'd be the sudden death of someone you know,

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:28.580
an old relative you realize you will have to say goodbye to soon.

00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:32.100
Or yes, finding out someone has a terminal illness.

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:35.940
There are times in life you encounter that can serve as a heavy reminder that,

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:41.540
no matter how far away it may be, this is an inevitable fate for all of us.

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:45.380
And, as morbid as that sounds, it doesn't have to be.

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:50.640
Sometimes that presence of death is the wake-up call we need to reflect on life.

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:53.300
And, the kind of mark we were able to leave.

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:55.620
In the same vain of attending someone's funeral,

00:09:55.620 --> 00:10:00.160
looking around and being able to see the invisible footprint of everyone who've left a mark on

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:01.900
in the time they've spent on this Earth.

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:07.640
This film was the story of how two people were able to leave a footprint on each other's lives.

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:10.040
In the short time they were able to have.

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:13.080
The death of a loved one, is never an easy thing to face

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:15.220
and everyone processes it differently.

00:10:15.220 --> 00:10:18.460
Sometimes you hear the news, or you're standing there at the funeral

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:20.500
and it hasn't properly hit you yet.

00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:22.920
You logically know they're not there anymore.

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:26.760
But it still feels like some ethereal dream you're going to wake up from

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:29.020
and everything is going to be as it was.

00:10:29.020 --> 00:10:33.120
But, there will be a point when the gravity of the reality truly hits you.

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:35.920
Maybe it's seeing their empty room for the first time.

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:38.280
Randomly getting a whiff of the scent they used to have.

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:41.020
Or feeling the grooves on the couch they used to sit on,

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:47.000
and knowing, you'll never see them sit in that same spot ever again.

00:10:47.260 --> 00:10:50.780
Sometimes it's not until that moment of crushing realization

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:57.360
that you're able to truly appreciate how deep a footprint that person was able to leave on your own life.

